{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And Moses heard, and fell, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say <b>and he heard</b>, since the verse already said ״וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֲלֵיהֶם״ — how could it even enter the mind that he did not hear? Further, one needs to know what this falling is. Now it appears that since the mockers did not complete the formulation of the intended proposal, the verse informs us that Moses understood the ultimate meaning of their words even though they did not spell them out. And its saying <b>and he fell</b>, etc., corresponds to what they said — that they exalt themselves over the congregation of HaShem, for they included Moses and Aaron in their words. Corresponding to what touched upon Moses, he fell on his face to show the ultimate degree of self-abasement, and made his soul like the dust of the earth, like a servant who prostrates himself before authority. And corresponding to the greatness of Aaron, he said <b>and he spoke to Korah</b>, etc., <b>in the morning</b>, etc. — meaning: you will not vindicate your words with this until you test whether the matter went out from me or whether the matter went out from HaShem."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:09:54.782796484Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}